<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13363"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Rocky<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Rocky<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, May 03, 2005 - 11:55 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Will the McNaughten Rule apply to this piece of trash? Phoenix how did you know that demon @&#42;&#42; was going to stand mute before the rest of the world did?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13363-->
<!--Post: 13364--><!--Time: 1115147779-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13364"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Elsewhere<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Elsewhere<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, May 03, 2005 - 12:16 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->OMG - please tell me this @&#42;&#42; isn&#39;t really going to try to pull off an &#34;insanity plea defense&#34; and Rocky - what is the McNaughten Rule? <BR>Phoenix - Rocky - you both know the law so well...is there a possibility that they may try for insanity? <BR>But he functioned in a &#34;normal&#34; world - outside of his killing for fun spree...how could he ever pass that &#34;Christian&#34; Life and working for Park CIty off and claim he was insane? <BR>I mean we all know he is INSANE...but how will that change things?  I won&#39;t be able to stand it if he gets away with an insanity plea.... <BR>Will he be able to be institutionalized? <BR>I can&#39;t stand it.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13364-->
<!--Post: 13365--><!--Time: 1115150030-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13365"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Rocky<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Rocky<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, May 03, 2005 - 12:53 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Else - Phoenix is the professor! I&#39;m just the paralegal that works in a civil practice, but I had a really good criminal law professor with a really good law dictionary. HA!  <BR> <BR>BTW-I&#39;m tired of the snow and the multi-car pile ups, and the tournaments gettin snowed out etc., how is your spring going? <BR> <BR>McNaghten Rule was the common law test of criminal responsibility. The person is unable to establish right from wrong. Then this rule was modified to include the Durham Rule which includes an accused is not criminally responsible if his unlawful act was the product of mental disease or defect. Oh yea, then there is the ALI Test which also provides that the terms mental disease or defect do not include an abnormaility manifested only by repeated criminal or otherwise anitsocial conduct. This language is used to exclude the sociopathic or psychopathic criminal. <BR> <BR>I don&#39;t know which standard above Kansas has to base insanity on. Each state uses a different standard. Phoenix? <BR> <BR>Then you have Mens Rea which means, a guilty mind, the mental state accompanying a forbidden act. The four criminal states of mind are: <BR>intentionally, knowingly, recklessly and grossly negligent. So, the state must prove that beyond a reasonable doubt that the required mental state coexisted with the doing of the proscribed act. <BR> <BR>If Demon @ss meets the standard used in Kansas to base insanity, then he couldn&#39;t have the mens rea to committ murder. Right Phoenix? <BR> <BR>Either way, all of the above will be argued.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13365-->
<!--Post: 13370--><!--Time: 1115162427-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13370"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Phoenix<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Phoenix<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->3-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, May 03, 2005 - 4:20 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Rocky: <BR> <BR>Sometimes I get lucky. <BR> <BR>The standard at the time of the homicides was M&#39;Naughten.  It probably incorporated into every criminal statute because it defined the state of mind which was exempt from criminal liability. <BR> <BR>Sounds like it&#39;s time for Prof. Phoenix&#39;s Criminal Law class on &#42;INSANITY: Theory and Tactics&#42; <BR> <BR>Phoenix<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13370-->
<!--Post: 13376--><!--Time: 1115165897-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13376"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Rocky<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Rocky<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, May 03, 2005 - 5:18 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Ok professor do your thing! Here&#39;s my question - From my understanding in most states there is a separate hearing that is specifically for the determination of sanity, does Kansas proceed in this light? If so, what happens if demon @&#42;&#42; is found to be insane by all the experts and not allowed to have a trial? If Kansas doesn&#39;t have a separate hearing, then as the State proceeds with a trial is there a time frame for demon @&#42;&#42; to change his plea and enter an insanity plea? <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13376-->
<!--Post: 13384--><!--Time: 1115183131-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13384"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Johno<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Johno<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, May 03, 2005 - 10:05 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Dennis Rader is not insane and I don&#39;t think his defensive team could come up with any credible pychonalysis showing that he is insane, suffers from paranoid schizophrenea or experiances auditory illusions. <BR>They more likely will go for the alter-ego defense. Incapacitated at the time of the murders by being under the influence of an alternative personality. <BR>In my opinion Dennis Rader is not mentally insane, he is morally insane. He is sociopathic and suffers from a disorder of the character not the mind. <BR>This is a personality disorder that led to his being a serial killer. He had a problem resolving frustrations from adolescense into adulthood. Thankfully most people can resolve emotional issues by themselves or with help. He could not and did not and instead chose to transfer his frustrations and inadequasies into a separate compartment of life where he could deal with his problems by becoming a serial killer. <BR>There may be a neurological disfunction in his brain that developed or was hereditary that led to him being incapable of resolving his personality disorder on his own but it is not insanity. <BR>I of course have no professional or educational background on this topic but this is my layman&#39;s opinion.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13384-->
<!--Post: 13399--><!--Time: 1115217774-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13399"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Elsewhere<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Elsewhere<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, May 04, 2005 - 7:42 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Johno - I aggree with your &#34;laymans&#34; opinion.  The demon @&#42;&#42; reminds me so much of Ted Bundy.  One difference, the lack of communication from Bundy and wanting the notirety &#40;sp?&#41; - BUT the arrogance is there and the mind set.  Thoroughly enjoying what they did.... <BR>Monsters. <BR>Prof - please keep us posted on the Kansas Laws for insanity...this jack demon @&#42;&#42; just seems to be up to something...there remains a sparkle in his eyes.... <BR>Rocky - OMG - I have never felt so strong about an individual...I want justice for the victims...I want him behind bars and suffering forever...<!--/Text--></td></tr>
