<!--Post: 13497--><!--Time: 1115399169-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13497"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Lostdog<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Lostdog<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, May 06, 2005 - 10:06 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->MS. FOULSTON: Are you Dennis Rader? <BR> <BR>THE DEFENDANT: Yes, ma&#39;am. <BR> <BR>MS. FOULSTON: Mr. Rader, I am District Attorney Nola Foulston. <BR> <BR>I am going to hand you what has been called the Notice of Intent to Request a Separate Sentencing, and this document has been prepared by the State and requests that on admission or adjudication of guilt on the crime of first degree murder of Dolores Davis, based upon the finding of premeditated murder, that the State does intend to request that in a separate sentencing proceeding to determine whether or not you should be required to serve a mandatory sentence of imprisonment of a term of 40 years. <BR> <BR>This is based upon the law called the Hard 40. <BR> <BR>The State has alleged that there are aggravating circumstances.  <BR> <BR>That you committed the crime in order to avoid or prevent a lawful arrest. <BR> <BR>And, No. 2, that you committed the crime in an especially heinous, atrocious or cruel manner. <BR> <BR>And that the term heinous means extremely wicked or shockingly evil. <BR> <BR>Atrocious means outrageously wicked, vile, cruel, including pitiless or designed to inflict a high degree of pain, utter indifference to, or enjoyment of, the suffering of others. <BR> <BR>We have also reviewed all the mitigating circumstances in this crime, and we find that there are no mitigating circumstances. <BR> <BR>This has been signed by me. It&#39;s also been certified by the Clerk of the District Court, Susan Tanner. It&#39;s also been filed with the Court as an official document, and I have a certificate of service showing that it has been personally served on you, sir. I am giving you this document today, and I am going to give a copy directly to your counsel. <BR> <BR>And now you have been served, sir. <BR> <BR>Thank you very much. <BR> <BR>THE DEFENDANT: Uh-huh. <BR> <BR>LOSTDOG: Uh-huh?... Uh-huh? No, no Dennis! <BR> <BR>Didn’t you mean to say: <BR> <BR>You’re welcome Ms. Foulston -- and ma’am let me thank you for finally concluding what has so far been the longest 51 seconds of my life. <BR>  <BR>When I was nodding my diseased noggin, I was not trying to indicate that I understood you… <BR>  <BR>I was merely attempting to get you to speed up your kindergarten teacher’s cadence so that I could: <BR>  <BR>&#40;1&#41; get the hell out of the courtroom before Charlie whipped a ninja star into my cerebral cortex  <BR> <BR>and &#40;2&#41; control my seething rage for just another five seconds. <BR> <BR>See, while you were defining those big words “haynus” and “atroshis” for me – I was fantasizing about leaping over this table and taking you down to the floor. <BR>  <BR>Then I would have tried my best to crush your larynx with my thumbs before this big bailiff behind me had a chance to interrupt. <BR> <BR>Good thing for you, that “good Dennis” was able to keep me in my seat. <BR> <BR>As soon as I’m back in the pod, I’ll write two poem &#40;sic&#41; about my experience today. <BR> <BR>The first poem will be nasty and mean. <BR>  <BR>The second one will be very sweet and Lutheran. <BR> <BR>I’ll mail them both to my cousin – who, I know for certain, will hand them over to KAKE. <BR> <BR>Then, after lights out, I’ll raise the curtain in the dark theater of my mind. <BR> <BR>I’ll visualize a new outcome to this day. <BR>  <BR>The scene is a dimly lit courtroom. It is empty, save for you and me. <BR>  <BR>I am sitting on the bench in a black robe and you request permission to approach. <BR> <BR>As soon as you st— <BR> <BR>LOSTDOG: Whoa Denny… ZIP IT! Zip it up, son. <BR>  <BR>I suppose “Uh-Huh” was the correct response after all. <BR>  <BR>In fact, “mute” is an even better choice. <BR>  <BR>By the way, as far as you jumping Ms Foulston,…  <BR>It looks to me like Nola is fully capable of whuppin’ your prison-prized, heinous heiny while simultaneously reciting the Hard 40 statute in Latin. <BR>  <BR>Yes, she could mess you up good – but she’ll just throw the book at you instead. <BR> <BR> <BR>Lost40   <BR>  <BR> <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13497-->
